@page "/components/fileupload"
@page "/components/file-upload"

<PageOutlet Url="components/fileupload"
            Title="FileUpload"
            Description="An upload component that sends files to a server endpoint over HTTP, with browse, drag-and-drop, paste, folder and camera selection, chunked transfers, progress, retries and validation." />

<DemoPage Name="FileUpload"
          Description="BitFileUpload is the transport half of working with files: it takes the files the user hands over and uploads them to a server endpoint over HTTP. Files arrive through the file dialog, a drag out of the OS onto the component or onto a drop zone of your own, a clipboard paste, a whole folder or the camera on a phone, and go out automatically on selection or on demand, as a single request per file or in chunks. Every file reports its own progress and can be paused, resumed, canceled, retried and removed from the server again, and selections are validated on arrival against size, count, extension, MIME type, duplicate, image dimension and custom rules. The upload and remove requests are customizable down to a per-request async provider, which is what makes an expiring token or a presigned URL work over a long transfer, and the whole control is themable, templatable and announced to screen readers. For plain file selection without any uploading, reach for the BitFileInput component instead."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubUrl="Inputs/FileUpload/BitFileUpload.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Inputs/FileUpload/BitFileUploadDemo.razor">
    <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
        <div>
            Files arrive three ways out of the box: the browse button opens the file dialog, a file dragged out of
            the OS can be dropped anywhere on the component, and a file on the clipboard can be pasted while the
            focus is inside it. Each selected file becomes an item with its name, its size and an upload button that
            sends it to the <b>UploadUrl</b>; while files hover over the component the browse button turns into a
            dashed drop indicator. During the upload the item shows a progress bar with pause and cancel buttons,
            and once it settles, a status message reports the outcome.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Drop zone" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode"
                 CodeFiles="@example2CodeFiles" Id="example2">
        <div>
            The two alternative routes switch off independently. <b>AllowDrop</b> controls dropping files out of the
            OS: turning it off shows a "no drop" cursor and still swallows the event, so the browser does not
            navigate away to the dropped file and lose the page state. <b>AllowPaste</b> controls pasting from the
            clipboard, which is only delivered while the focus is inside the component - so the browse button has to
            be focused first. Both take effect immediately when toggled at runtime.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitCheckbox @bind-Value="allowDrop" Label="AllowDrop" />
        <br />
        <BitCheckbox @bind-Value="allowPaste" Label="AllowPaste" />
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select, drop or paste files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" AllowDrop="allowDrop" AllowPaste="allowPaste" />

        <br /><br /><br />

        <div>
            The component itself is always a drop zone, and <b>DropZoneSelector</b> nominates more of them: any
            element matching the selector takes a drop as well, so a whole form, a card or the page becomes the
            target while the browse button stays where it is. The element is matched when the drag reaches it, so
            one rendered later works with nothing to re-wire, and while a drag is in flight every zone - this
            component included - carries <b>Classes.Dragging</b> and <b>Styles.Dragging</b>, which is the hook the
            card below highlights itself with. Having the focus inside a zone also lets a paste land in the
            component.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div id="fileupload-drop-zone" class="drop-zone">
            <div>Drop files anywhere in this card</div>
            <BitFileUpload Label="...or browse for them" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple
                           DropZoneSelector="#fileupload-drop-zone"
                           Classes="@(new() { Dragging = "drop-zone-dragging" })" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Description" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
        <div>
            <b>Description</b> renders a hint under the browse button and wires it to that button through
            aria-describedby, so a screen reader reads the constraints out with the button instead of leaving them
            to be discovered by trial and error. Spell out there what <b>Accept</b>, <b>AllowedExtensions</b> and
            <b>MaxSize</b> actually enforce. <b>DescriptionTemplate</b> takes over when the hint needs markup of its
            own, and <b>Classes.Description</b> / <b>Styles.Description</b> restyle it.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Browse for a document" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Accept=".pdf,.docx" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 5"
                       Description="PDF or DOCX, up to 5 MB." />
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Browse for an image" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Accept="image/*" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 2">
            <DescriptionTemplate>
                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--Info" />
                <span>Images only. Up to <b>2 MB</b>.</span>
            </DescriptionTemplate>
        </BitFileUpload>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Removable" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
        <div>
            <b>ShowRemoveButton</b> puts a remove button on each settled file item. A file whose bytes never reached
            the server is simply dropped from the view, while one that has (partially or fully) uploaded is deleted
            from the server through a DELETE request to the <b>RemoveUrl</b>, carrying the file name as a query
            string and the file id in the <b>BIT_FILE_ID</b> header; a non-success response marks the file with the
            <b>FailedRemoveMessage</b> instead of pretending it is gone. <b>RemoveButtonTitle</b> localizes the
            tooltip and the accessible label. Removing with the keyboard keeps the focus where the list is - on the
            item that takes the removed one's place, or on the browse button once the list empties - instead of
            dropping it on the document body with the button that was pressed.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Selection behavior" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
        <div>
            Five parameters decide what a selection does to the list. <b>Multiple</b> lets the dialog, a drop or a
            paste carry several files at once, each uploading independently. <b>AutoUpload</b> starts sending them
            the moment they arrive, skipping the per-file upload button - the pattern for an avatar picker or an
            attachment area, where selecting already expresses the intent to upload. <b>Append</b> adds a new
            selection to the end of the list instead of replacing it, keeping the upload state of what is already
            there, so a batch can be built up over several rounds. <b>AutoReset</b> does the opposite for browsing:
            it empties the list just before the dialog opens, even if the dialog is then cancelled.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Building a batch up makes it easy to pick the same file twice, so turning <b>AllowDuplicates</b> off
            rejects a file matching one already in the list - by name, size and last modified time, as close to an
            identity as the browser exposes - with the <b>DuplicateErrorMessage</b> under it. The rejection is not
            final: remove the original and the copy becomes eligible again, since the rule is re-evaluated over the
            whole list on every change.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>Multiple, auto-uploaded:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple AutoUpload />
        <br /><br />
        <div>Appending, without duplicates - try selecting the same file twice:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple Append
                       AllowDuplicates="false" ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>AutoReset - every browse starts from an empty list:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" AutoReset />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Validation" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
        <div>
            Every rule runs at selection time, whichever way the file arrived. A file that fails one is marked as
            not allowed with its message under it and is never sent - and it stays visible, so the user sees exactly
            what was rejected and why instead of it disappearing silently.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>MaxSize</b> and <b>MinSize</b> bound each file in bytes; <b>MaxTotalSize</b> bounds what the list adds
            up to, which is the limit that matches a per-request or per-user quota; <b>MaxCount</b> caps how many
            files the list holds. Only files that pass the other rules consume the total-size budget or a count slot,
            so a file rejected for its type never pushes a good file over the cap. All three list-level rules are
            re-evaluated on every change, so removing a file takes the next one waiting behind it back in.
            <b>MaxSizeErrorMessage</b> and its siblings carry the wording.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>FileValidator</b> runs last, for anything the built-in rules cannot express. Returning a message
            rejects the file, returning null accepts it, and a validator that throws invalidates only its own file,
            using the exception message as the text.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>Max 1 MB per file, with a custom message:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1"
                       MaxSizeErrorMessage="The file is too big! Please select a file smaller than 1 MB."
                       Description="Up to 1 MB per file." />
        <br /><br />
        <div>Min 1 KB per file:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" MinSize="1024" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>At most 3 files and 2 MB across the batch:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple Append
                       MaxCount="3" MaxTotalSize="1024 * 1024 * 2" ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       Description="Up to 3 files, 2 MB in total." />
        <br /><br />
        <div>A custom rule - empty (zero-byte) files are turned away:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple FileValidator="@ValidateEmptyFile" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accept & AllowedExtensions" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
        <div>
            The two work at different moments. <b>Accept</b> goes straight to the accept attribute of the underlying
            input, which only hints the <i>file dialog</i> to pre-filter what it offers - a drop, a paste or a "show
            all files" switch walks right past it. <b>AllowedExtensions</b> is the actual rule: it checks every file
            however it arrived and marks the mismatches as not allowed with the
            <b>NotAllowedExtensionErrorMessage</b>.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Its entries are matched leniently - an extension with or without its leading dot, in any casing - and an
            entry containing a slash is treated as a MIME type, with a trailing wildcard like <b>image/*</b> covering
            a whole group. It also generates the accept attribute when <b>Accept</b> is not set, which is why setting
            it alone is usually all you need; set <b>Accept</b> explicitly only when the dialog filter should differ
            from the rule.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>Accept only (the dialog filters, but a dropped file of any type is still taken and uploaded):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Accept=".png,.jpg" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>AllowedExtensions (the rule, which also generates the accept attribute):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       AllowedExtensions="@(new List<string> { ".gif",".jpg",".mp4" })" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>MIME types (every image plus PDFs):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       AllowedExtensions="@(new List<string> { "image/*", "application/pdf" })" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Directory & Capture" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
        <div>
            <b>Directory</b> turns the picker into a folder picker, and every file inside the chosen folder - and
            inside its subfolders, at any depth - lands in the list as a separate upload. It does the same for a drop,
            walking a dropped folder into its contents rather than ignoring it, which a plain file input cannot do.
            The browser warns before handing a whole folder over, and the usual validations still apply to every file
            that comes out of it, so pairing it with <b>AllowedExtensions</b>, <b>MaxCount</b> or <b>MaxTotalSize</b>
            is what keeps a stray folder from turning into a thousand uploads.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>Capture</b> asks a phone or tablet to open the camera or the microphone instead of the file browser:
            <b>"user"</b> for the front (selfie) camera and <b>"environment"</b> for the rear one. Which one is
            honored is up to the device, and a desktop browser ignores it and opens the usual dialog. Combine it with
            <b>Accept</b> to say what kind of capture is wanted and with <b>AutoUpload</b> so the shot goes out the
            moment it is taken.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop a folder" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Directory Multiple
                       MaxCount="20" Description="Up to 20 files out of the chosen folder." />
        <br /><br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Take a photo" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Accept="image/*" Capture="environment" AutoUpload
                       Description="Opens the rear camera on a mobile device." />
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Record a video" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Accept="video/*" Capture="user"
                       Description="Opens the front camera on a mobile device." />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Preview & image dimensions" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
        <div>
            <b>ShowPreview</b> puts a thumbnail of every selected image at the head of its item, so the user can
            confirm they picked the right picture before a byte goes out. It is produced in the browser from an
            object URL - nothing is uploaded to render it - and the URL is handed back as soon as the file is removed
            or the component is reset, which is what keeps a long-lived page from holding every picture ever picked.
            <b>BitFileInfo.PreviewUrl</b> exposes the same URL to a custom <b>FileViewTemplate</b>. Only an image has
            a thumbnail of its own, so every other kind of file takes a glyph of its type - read off the content type,
            falling back to the extension - in a box of exactly the same size, which is what keeps the names of a
            mixed list lined up along one edge.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>A mixed selection - pick an image, a PDF and an archive to compare:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple ShowPreview
                       ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl" />
        <br />
        <div>
            A limit in bytes is a poor way to say "nothing bigger than 4K": what matters for an image is its pixels.
            <b>ReadImageDimensions</b> decodes every selected image in the browser and fills the <b>Width</b> and
            <b>Height</b> of its <b>BitFileInfo</b> before the validations run, so a <b>FileValidator</b> can turn an
            image away for its dimensions without a single byte leaving the machine. Decoding is not free, which is
            why it is off by default and runs a few images at a time. Non-image files, and images the browser cannot
            decode, keep both values null - a validator has to read that as "unknown" rather than as a failure.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>Preview with a dimension rule (between 200x200 and 4000x4000):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop images" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple ShowPreview
                       Accept="image/*" ReadImageDimensions FileValidator="@ValidateImageDimensions"
                       ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       Description="Images between 200x200 and 4000x4000 pixels." />
        <br /><br />
        <div>
            Rendering the dimensions in a custom item, with the <BitLink Href="#example17">FileViewTemplate</BitLink>
            covered further down:
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop images" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple
                       Accept="image/*" ReadImageDimensions>
            <FileViewTemplate Context="file">
                <div class="dimensions-item">
                    <span>@file.Name</span>
                    <span>@(file.Width is null ? "unknown size" : $"{file.Width} x {file.Height}")</span>
                </div>
            </FileViewTemplate>
        </BitFileUpload>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
        <div>
            Every stage surfaces through a callback: <b>OnChange</b> whenever a file's status changes, <b>OnInvalid</b>
            right after it whenever a selection carries rejected files - handy for a summary or for blocking a submit
            button - <b>OnUploading</b> right before a file's first request (the natural place to attach per-file HTTP
            headers), <b>OnProgress</b> on every progress tick, <b>OnUploadComplete</b> / <b>OnUploadFailed</b> when a
            file settles, <b>OnRemoveComplete</b> / <b>OnRemoveFailed</b> for removals, and <b>OnAllUploadsComplete</b>
            once the whole batch has reached a terminal state. Try a file over 1 MB to see <b>OnInvalid</b> at work.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Each callback receives the <b>BitFileInfo</b> of the file, which carries what the server said about it:
            <b>ResponseStatus</b> is the HTTP status code of its last upload or removal response - 0 when the request
            never reached the server at all - and <b>Message</b> is the response body. That is what tells an expired
            token (401) from a payload the server refused (413) from a network that dropped, so a failure can be
            answered instead of only reported.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1"
                       OnAllUploadsComplete="@(() => onAllUploadsCompleteText = "All files are uploaded")"
                       OnInvalid="@(files => onInvalidText = $"{files.Length} file(s) rejected: {string.Join(", ", files.Select(f => f.Name))}")"
                       OnUploadFailed="@(info => onUploadFailedText = $"{info.Name} failed with status {info.ResponseStatus}")"
                       OnUploading="@(info => info.HttpHeaders = new Dictionary<string, string> { {"key1", "value1"} })" />
        <br />
        <div>@onAllUploadsCompleteText</div>
        <div>@onInvalidText</div>
        <div>@onUploadFailedText</div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Chunked" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
        <div>
            <b>ChunkedUpload</b> slices each file and sends it as a series of sequential requests instead of one
            monolithic one, which is what makes pausing, resuming and recovering large uploads practical: a paused or
            failed file resumes from the last successfully uploaded chunk, so a dropped connection costs one chunk
            instead of the whole transfer. <b>ChunkSize</b> pins the size of each slice in bytes, while
            <b>AutoChunkSize</b> adapts it to the observed connection speed between 512 KB and 10 MB - large enough
            on a fast link to keep the request overhead negligible, small enough on a slow one to keep the progress
            moving and a retry cheap.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Every chunk request carries what the server needs to reassemble the file: the <b>BIT_FILE_ID</b> header
            correlating the chunks of one file, and the byte range both as the standard <b>Content-Range</b> header
            (<b>bytes from-to/total</b>) and as the plain <b>BIT_CHUNK_FROM</b>, <b>BIT_CHUNK_TO</b> and
            <b>BIT_FILE_SIZE</b> headers for handlers that would rather not parse it. Writing each chunk at its own
            offset rather than blindly appending is what makes the server correct in the face of a retried chunk.
            CORS treats these as custom request headers, so a cross-origin endpoint has to allow them.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@ChunkedUploadUrl" ChunkedUpload />
        <br /><br />
        <div>Adaptive chunk sizing:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@ChunkedUploadUrl" ChunkedUpload AutoChunkSize />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Http requests" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
        <div>
            Static <b>UploadRequestHttpHeaders</b> / <b>RemoveRequestHttpHeaders</b> and
            <b>UploadRequestQueryStrings</b> / <b>RemoveRequestQueryStrings</b> attach headers and query strings to
            the respective requests, and <b>UploadRequestFormFields</b> adds extra multipart fields next to the file
            content itself - a target folder, an album id, a caption - for the endpoints that read their metadata
            from the form rather than from the URL.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       UploadRequestQueryStrings="@(new Dictionary<string, string>{ {"qs1", "qsValue1" } })"
                       UploadRequestHttpHeaders="@(new Dictionary<string, string>{ {"header1", "value1" } })"
                       UploadRequestFormFields="@(new Dictionary<string, string>{ {"folder", "invoices" } })"
                       RemoveRequestQueryStrings="@(new Dictionary<string, string>{ {"qs2", "qsValue2" } })"
                       RemoveRequestHttpHeaders="@(new Dictionary<string, string>{ {"header2", "value2" } })" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>
            Each has an async <b>...Provider</b> counterpart, and the difference is not only that it is a function:
            a provider is invoked before <i>every single request</i> - each file, and each chunk of a chunked file -
            while the static values are fixed once, when the files are selected. That is what makes a provider the
            right place for anything that expires: an access token that has to be fresh when the request goes out, or
            a presigned URL good for one upload only, which <b>UploadUrlProvider</b> mints for the endpoint itself. A
            value that never changes belongs in the static parameter, since a provider costs an await per request.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple ChunkedUpload
                       UploadRequestHttpHeadersProvider="@GetFreshAuthHeaders" />
        <br />
        <div>Requests so far: @tokenRequestCount</div>
        <br /><br />
        <div>
            The transport is configurable too: <b>UploadRequestHttpMethod</b> switches the verb ("POST" by default -
            a PUT is what presigned URL flows usually expect), <b>UploadFormFieldName</b> renames the form field
            carrying the file content ("file" by default), <b>WithCredentials</b> sends cookies and authorization
            headers on cross-origin requests, and <b>UploadTimeout</b> fails a request - each file or chunk - that
            takes longer than the given time. <b>RemoveRequestHttpMethod</b> does the same for the removal, whose
            "DELETE" some APIs would rather receive as a "POST".
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       UploadRequestHttpMethod="POST"
                       UploadFormFieldName="file"
                       RemoveRequestHttpMethod="DELETE"
                       ShowRemoveButton
                       WithCredentials
                       UploadTimeout="TimeSpan.FromMinutes(10)" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Retry" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
        <div>
            <b>AutoRetries</b> retries a failed upload automatically - up to the given number of times per file,
            waiting <b>AutoRetryDelay</b> before each attempt - and in the chunked mode every retry resumes from the
            last uploaded chunk. Pausing or canceling a file while a retry is waiting out its delay wins over that
            retry, so the outcome the user asked for is the one that stands.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The budget is only spent where a second try could go differently: network errors, timeouts, 408, 429 and
            the 5xx server errors are retried, while the other 4xx - a 404 pointing nowhere, a 413 that is simply too
            large, a 401 - settle the file immediately rather than repeating an identical doomed request.
            <b>ShouldAutoRetry</b> replaces that rule with your own, receiving the file and the status code.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Hammering a server that is already struggling with evenly spaced retries is what turns a blip into an
            outage, so <b>AutoRetryDelayProvider</b> replaces the fixed delay with one worked out per attempt - it
            receives the file, whose <b>ResponseStatus</b> says what came back, and the number of the attempt about
            to be made, and the usual answer is an exponential backoff with a little jitter.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Once the budget is exhausted the file settles as failed and the item offers a retry button - distinct
            from the upload button of a never-started file in both icon and wording, so a second attempt never reads
            as a first one. Pressing it tries again with a fresh budget, exactly like calling <b>Upload(file)</b>
            does. <b>RetryIcon</b>, <b>RetryIconName</b> and <b>RetryButtonTitle</b> restyle and localize it, falling
            back to their upload counterparts when unset.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       AutoRetries="2" AutoRetryDelay="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>
            A non-existing endpoint (404): the built-in rule does not retry it, so the file fails at once and offers
            the manual retry button.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@NonExistingUploadUrl"
                       AutoRetries="2" AutoRetryDelay="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)"
                       RetryButtonTitle="Try again" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>The same endpoint with a <b>ShouldAutoRetry</b> that insists: watch it retry twice before settling.</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@NonExistingUploadUrl"
                       AutoRetries="2" AutoRetryDelay="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)"
                       ShouldAutoRetry="@((file, status) => true)"
                       RetryButtonTitle="Try again" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>The same, backing off 1s, 2s then 4s instead of retrying on a fixed beat:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@NonExistingUploadUrl"
                       AutoRetries="3"
                       ShouldAutoRetry="@((file, status) => true)"
                       AutoRetryDelayProvider="@BackOff"
                       RetryButtonTitle="Try again" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="ConcurrentUploads" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
        <div>
            By default every file of a batch goes on the wire at once, which is the fastest option on a healthy
            connection but not always the kindest: browsers keep only a handful of connections open per host, and a
            server behind a rate limit would rather not meet fifty simultaneous uploads. <b>ConcurrentUploads</b>
            caps how many files may be in flight; the rest wait in a queue in selection order and each starts the
            moment a slot frees up - whether the file holding it completed, failed, was canceled or merely paused.
            Set it to 0 (the default) to start everything at once.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The cap counts files, not requests: a chunked file holds its slot for the whole run of its chunks rather
            than going back to the end of the queue between two of them. The queue only governs the start of an
            upload, so a file started explicitly through <b>Upload(file)</b> waits its turn too.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            A waiting file says so instead of looking like one nobody asked to upload: its item shows the
            <b>QueuedUploadMessage</b>, drops the start button and keeps a cancel button so it can be called off
            before its turn comes. <b>BitFileInfo.IsQueued</b> exposes the same state to a custom
            <b>FileViewTemplate</b>.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>Two files at a time:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple ConcurrentUploads="2" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>One file at a time (a strictly sequential queue), with a translated waiting message:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple AutoUpload
                       ConcurrentUploads="1" QueuedUploadMessage="In the queue…" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Overall progress, speed & ETA" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
        <div>
            Besides the per-file progress, the component aggregates the batch: <b>TotalSize</b> and
            <b>TotalUploadedSize</b> expose the combined byte counts of the files actually being uploaded - rejected
            and removed files do not count - and <b>OverallUploadProgress</b> turns them into one percentage,
            weighting every file by its size.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            A percentage answers "how far along"; what a user waiting on a large upload wants is "how much longer".
            Every running file measures the speed of the request in flight and exposes <b>UploadSpeed</b> (bytes per
            second) and <b>RemainingTime</b> on its <b>BitFileInfo</b>. Both are null while a file is not on the wire
            - before it starts, once it settles, and for the first fraction of a second, where a reading taken over
            almost no time would say nothing but a huge number. In the chunked mode the speed is measured over the
            chunk in flight, so it follows a connection that changes mid-file instead of averaging it away.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The batch has the same two numbers: <b>TotalUploadSpeed</b> adds up every file on the wire - what the
            connection as a whole is carrying - and <b>OverallRemainingTime</b> divides the bytes still to go by it.
            Both are null while nothing is uploading, which is the difference between "not moving" and "moving at
            zero". All of them update on every progress tick, so rendering them is a matter of re-reading them from
            <b>OnProgress</b>.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload @ref="speedFileUpload" Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple
                       OnChange="@(_ => StateHasChanged())" OnProgress="@(_ => StateHasChanged())" />
        <br />
        <BitProgress Percent="@(speedFileUpload?.OverallUploadProgress ?? 0)" ShowPercentNumber />
        <br />
        @foreach (var file in SpeedFiles)
        {
            <div>
                @file.Name -
                @(file.UploadSpeed is null ? "-" : $"{file.UploadSpeed / 1024:N0} KB/s")
                (@(file.RemainingTime is null ? "-" : $"{file.RemainingTime:mm\\:ss} left"))
            </div>
        }
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>
                Batch: @(speedFileUpload?.TotalUploadSpeed is null ? "idle" : $"{speedFileUpload.TotalUploadSpeed / 1024:N0} KB/s")
                (@(speedFileUpload?.OverallRemainingTime is null ? "-" : $"{speedFileUpload.OverallRemainingTime:mm\\:ss} left"))
            </b>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="File list display" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example16CsharpCode" Id="example16">
        <div>
            <b>FileSizeFormatter</b> takes over the built-in humanizer, which speaks English and counts in binary
            units: it receives a size in bytes and returns whatever text should appear - a localized unit name, a
            different rounding, or the decimal base some platforms prefer.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple
                       FileSizeFormatter="@(size => $"{size:N0} bytes")" />
        <br /><br />
        <div>
            <b>HideFileView</b> takes the list away without taking the files with it: they are still selected,
            validated, uploaded and reported through <b>Files</b> and the callbacks - they are simply not drawn. That
            is what the surrounding page needs when it shows the attachments in its own layout, or when the uploader
            has to collapse into a single button. Reach for <b>FileViewTemplate</b> instead when the list should stay
            where it is and only look different.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload @ref="hiddenViewFileUpload" Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       Multiple AutoUpload HideFileView
                       OnChange="@(_ => StateHasChanged())" OnProgress="@(_ => StateHasChanged())" />
        <br />
        @if (HiddenViewFiles.Any())
        {
            <ul>
                @foreach (var file in HiddenViewFiles)
                {
                    <li>@file.Name - @file.Status</li>
                }
            </ul>
            <div>Overall progress: @(hiddenViewFileUpload?.OverallUploadProgress ?? 0)%</div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div>No file selected yet.</div>
        }
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Templates" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example17CsharpCode" Id="example17">
        <div>
            <b>LabelTemplate</b> replaces the browse button - here with a full drop-zone panel that hides itself once
            files are selected - and <b>FileViewTemplate</b> replaces each item of the list, receiving the file info
            as its context with its name, size, progress, speed and status. Replacing the browse button also replaces
            the dashed drop indicator that lives on it, so a custom label should bring its own drag feedback:
            <b>Classes.Dragging</b> puts a class on the root element for as long as files hover over the component.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Two things are worth carrying over from the default UI. The controls should be real <b>button</b>
            elements rather than clickable divs, so they stay reachable with Tab and can be activated with Enter or
            Space, and each should name the file it acts on through an aria-label - a list of identical "Remove"
            buttons tells a screen reader user nothing about which file they are on. And since the context is the
            file itself, call the per-file overloads: <b>Upload(file)</b> and <b>RemoveFile(file)</b> act on that one
            file, while their no-argument forms sweep through the whole batch.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <BitFileUpload @ref="bitFileUpload" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                           Classes="@(new() { Dragging = "custom-drop-zone" })">
                <LabelTemplate>
                    @if (FileUploadIsEmpty())
                    {
                        @* a real button rather than a clickable div, so the drop zone is reachable with Tab
                           and activated with Enter or Space like the built-in browse button it replaces. *@
                        <button type="button" class="browse-file" @onclick="() => bitFileUpload.Browse()">
                            <div class="browse-file-header">
                                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--CloudUpload" />
                                <div>
                                    Drag and drop or
                                </div>
                                <div>
                                    <strong>
                                        Browse file
                                    </strong>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="browse-file-footer">
                                <div>
                                    Max file size: 2 MB
                                </div>
                                <div>
                                    Supported file types: jpg, jpeg, png, bmp
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </button>
                    }
                </LabelTemplate>
                <FileViewTemplate Context="file">
                    @if (file.Status != BitFileUploadStatus.Removed)
                    {
                        <div class="file-list">
                            <div class="file-info">
                                <div class="file-info-ico">
                                    <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--FileImage" />
                                </div>
                                <div class="file-info-data">
                                    <div class="file-info-title">
                                        <div class="file-info-name">@file.Name</div>
                                        @* each action names the file it acts on and applies to that file
                                           alone, which is what the context of the template is there for. *@
                                        <div class="file-info-btns">
                                            <button type="button" aria-label="@($"Upload {file.Name}")" @onclick="() => bitFileUpload.Upload(file)">
                                                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--CloudUpload upload-ico" />
                                            </button>
                                            <button type="button" aria-label="@($"Remove {file.Name}")" @onclick="() => bitFileUpload.RemoveFile(file)">
                                                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--ChromeClose remove-ico" />
                                            </button>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                    @if (file.Status is BitFileUploadStatus.InProgress or BitFileUploadStatus.Pending)
                                    {
                                        var fileUploadPercent = GetFileUploadPercent(file);
                                        <div class="file-info-subtitle">@GetFileUploadSize(file) - @fileUploadPercent%</div>
                                        <div class="file-info-progressbar-container">
                                            <div class="file-info-progressbar" role="progressbar" style="width:@fileUploadPercent%;" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="@fileUploadPercent"></div>
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <div class="@(file.Status == BitFileUploadStatus.Completed ? "file-info-s-msg" : "file-info-e-msg")">@GetUploadMessageStr(file)</div>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="file-list-footer">
                                <div>
                                    Max file size: 2 MB
                                </div>
                                <div>
                                    Supported file types: jpg, jpeg, png, bmp
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </FileViewTemplate>
            </BitFileUpload>
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitButton OnClick="HandleUploadOnClick">Upload</BitButton>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Public API" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example18CsharpCode" Id="example18">
        <div>
            The component can be driven entirely from code: <b>Browse</b> opens the file dialog (here the built-in
            button is hidden with <b>HideLabel</b> and replaced by external BitButtons), <b>Upload</b> starts the
            upload, <b>PauseUpload</b> and <b>CancelUpload</b> stop it, <b>RemoveFile</b> removes files and
            <b>Reset</b> clears the whole state. Each takes a single file or, with no argument, the whole batch.
            <b>Files</b> exposes the current selection with each file's status and progress, and <b>UploadStatus</b>
            reports the batch as a whole - pending until the first upload starts, in progress from then on, and
            completed once every file has reached a terminal state.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The difference between the two ways of stopping is what happens next. <b>PauseUpload</b> applies to the
            files actually on their way: it aborts the in-flight request and keeps the bytes that made it, so
            <b>Upload</b> resumes from there - from the last completed chunk in the chunked mode - and it takes a
            file still waiting in the concurrency queue back out of that queue. A file nobody asked to upload has
            nothing to pause, so it is left as it is.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>CancelUpload</b> settles the file as canceled instead, and does so immediately whether it was running,
            queued, paused or merely selected. Files that have already settled are left alone: a completed upload
            cannot be taken back from the server by anything on this side, and calling off a file that already failed
            would only replace the reason it failed with a less useful one. A canceled file can be started again
            later, and its item offers the retry button for exactly that.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload @ref="bitFileUploadWithBrowseFile" HideLabel Multiple
                       UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl" />
        <br />
        <div class="api-buttons">
            <BitButton OnClick="HandleBrowseFileOnClick">Browse files</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => bitFileUploadWithBrowseFile.Upload()">Upload all</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => bitFileUploadWithBrowseFile.PauseUpload()">Pause all</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => bitFileUploadWithBrowseFile.CancelUpload()">Cancel all</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => bitFileUploadWithBrowseFile.Reset()">Reset</BitButton>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19">
        <div>
            The whole control is operable from the keyboard and every interactive part shows a focus ring: the browse
            button is a real button - Tab to it, Enter or Space to open the dialog - and each file item's upload,
            pause, cancel and remove buttons follow it in the tab order, each with an aria-label naming the file it
            acts on. An action that takes its own button out of the DOM hands the focus on rather than dropping it:
            pausing moves it to the resume button that replaced it, and removing a file moves it to the item that
            takes its place, or to the browse button when the list empties.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The list carries list and listitem roles and is named by <b>FileListAriaLabel</b>; the progress bar
            exposes its percentage through the progressbar role; and a visually hidden live region announces the
            selection and every upload outcome, so a screen reader user hears how many files were taken and which
            uploads succeeded or failed without going looking. <b>AnnouncementProvider</b> replaces that built-in
            English sentence - the place to translate it or word it around your domain - and <b>AriaLabel</b> labels
            the browse button when its visible text does not say enough on its own.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl"
                       AriaLabel="Select a document to upload" />
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Multiple
                       ShowRemoveButton RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       FileListAriaLabel="Documents to upload"
                       AnnouncementProvider="@AnnounceUploads" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Variant" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20">
        <div>
            The browse button is the visual weight the component carries, and <b>Variant</b> decides how much of it
            there is. <b>Fill</b> - the default - paints it in a solid block of the
            <BitLink Href="#example23">Color</BitLink>, for when uploading is the main thing the screen is for.
            <b>Outline</b> keeps only the rule around it, for an uploader among other form fields. <b>Text</b> drops
            both rule and fill, for a secondary affordance. The drop indicator and the file list follow along, and a
            disabled component drops the role colors entirely for the neutral disabled look, whichever variant it
            uses.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="variant" Horizontal TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitVariant>" TValue="BitVariant">
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Fill" Value="BitVariant.Fill" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Outline" Value="BitVariant.Outline" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Text" Value="BitVariant.Text" />
        </BitChoiceGroup>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Select or drag and drop files" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Variant="variant" />
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Disabled" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" Variant="variant" IsEnabled="false" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Preloaded files" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example21CsharpCode" Id="example21">
        <div>
            A form editing a record usually opens with attachments already on the server, and
            <b>PreloadedFiles</b> is how they get into the list beside the ones the user is about to add.
            They carry no content on this side, so they are never uploaded: each starts out completed, shows
            its size alone rather than a transfer that just ran, says so through
            <b>PreloadedFileMessage</b>, and counts towards <b>MaxCount</b> and <b>MaxTotalSize</b> like any
            other file. Give each one the server's own id as its <b>FileId</b> - that is what travels in the
            <b>BIT_FILE_ID</b> header when the remove button deletes it through the
            <BitLink Href="#example4">RemoveUrl</BitLink> - and a <b>PreviewUrl</b> pointing at a thumbnail
            the server serves, which <b>ShowPreview</b> renders like a locally generated one.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The list is rebuilt whenever the files in the collection change - which files, by their
            <b>FileId</b>, not which array they arrive in - so a record that loads late lands in the list
            while one written inline in the markup does not undo the user's edits on every render. The
            instances are adopted rather than copied, so each one's <b>Status</b> is where to read what
            became of it - <b>Removed</b> for the ones the user deleted - when the form is saved. They also
            survive what clears the rest: browsing without <b>Append</b> replaces the selection and
            <b>Reset</b> empties it, and both leave the record's own files where they are.
        </div>
        <br />
        <BitFileUpload Label="Add more attachments" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" RemoveUrl="@RemoveUrl"
                       Multiple Append ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                       PreloadedFiles="@attachments"
                       PreloadedFileMessage="Saved with this record"
                       Description="Remove an existing attachment or add new ones." />
        <br />
        <BitButton OnClick="LoadAttachments">Reload the record</BitButton>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
        <div>
            <b>BitParams</b> carries a <b>BitFileUploadParams</b> down to every file upload under it, so a form, a
            card or a whole page sets the shared endpoint, the shared limits and the shared look once instead of on
            every uploader. What it carries is a default and not an override: an uploader that writes a parameter for
            itself keeps its own value, and only what it left unset is filled in from the cascade - which is what
            lets one step out of the group without the group having to know about it. Every parameter is on the
            params object and each is filled in on its own, so one uploader can take its <b>UploadUrl</b> from the
            cascade and its <b>MaxSize</b> from its own markup.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitParams Parameters="@fileUploadParams">
                <BitFileUpload Label="Takes the endpoint, the limits and the look from the cascade" />
                <BitFileUpload Label="So does this one, without repeating any of it" />
                <BitFileUpload Label="Its own Variant and MaxSize, the cascaded rest"
                               Variant="BitVariant.Fill" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1" />
            </BitParams>
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitFileUpload Label="Outside the cascade, and back to the defaults" UploadUrl="@UploadUrl" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

